ith her family during World War II." data-title="The Diary of a Young Girl" type="button">Details</button><a class="btn" href="#story-29">Read Story</a></div></div></article><article class="book-card" data-genre="Science" data-title="a brief history of time stephen hawking"><div class="cover" style="--h:353"><span class="cover-title">A Brief History of Time</span><img alt="Cover of A Brief History of Time" data-img="covers/30"/></div><div class="book-info"><h3>A Brief History of Time</h3><p class="author">Stephen Hawking</p><p class="genre">Science / Nonfiction</p><div class="card-actions"><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Stephen Hawking" data-cover="" data-ck="30" data-genre="Science / Nonfiction" data-href="#story-30" data-synopsis="Stephen Hawking introduces major ideas about the universe, including black holes, space, time, and the origins of the cosmos." data-title="A Brief History of Time" type="button">Details</button><a class="btn" href="#story-30">Read Story</a></div></div></article></section><p class="empty" hidden="" id="empty">No books match your search. Clear the filters to see all 30.</p></section><section id="story-01" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:0"><span class="cover-title">Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone</span><img alt="Cover of Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone" data-img="covers/01"/></div><div><h1>Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone</h1><p class="author">by J.K. Rowling</p><p class="genre">Fantasy</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="01" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="J.K. Rowling" data-cover="" data-ck="01" data-genre="Fantasy" data-href="#story-01" data-synopsis="A young boy discovers that he is a wizard and begins studying magic at Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry." data-title="Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A young boy discovers that he is a wizard and begins studying magic at Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Letter on the Doormat</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Letter on the Doormat" data-img="stories/01"/><figcaption>The Letter on the Doormat</figcaption></figure><p>Mina found the envelope wedged under the bakery door, addressed in green ink to 'The Girl Who Fixes Clocks.' She almost threw it away. Then the wax seal warmed in her hand and the clock above the counter began ticking backwards.</p><p>By nightfall a tall stranger with a lantern was knocking. 'Pack light,' he said. 'The school does not like latecomers, and the staircases do not wait.' Mina grabbed her tools and stepped into the dark.</p><p>The stranger introduced himself as Mr. Alder and led her to a train platform that was not on any map. A scarlet engine hissed there, steaming with purple smoke. Mina climbed aboard with her heart racing.</p><p>Inside, a boy with a toad and a girl with a stack of books argued over a seat. 'Are you a first-year too?' the girl asked. Mina nodded, and for the first time all year, she did not feel alone.</p><p>The school stood on a cliff above a black lake, its windows glowing like a thousand patient eyes. The first-years crossed in little boats while a stern headmistress called out names. Mina gripped the rail and felt every clock in her pocket tick faster.</p><p>In the Great Hall, a tall door rose before them. 'Step inside,' said the headmistress. 'The Hall will decide where you belong.' Mina walked in, and every clock on every wall began chiming together. Someone gasped. Someone laughed. Mina, at last, felt at home.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><span></span><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-02">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-02" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:37"><span class="cover-title">The Hunger Games</span><img alt="Cover of The Hunger Games" data-img="covers/02"/></div><div><h1>The Hunger Games</h1><p class="author">by Suzanne Collins</p><p class="genre">Dystopian / Science Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="02" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Suzanne Collins" data-cover="" data-ck="02" data-genre="Dystopian / Science Fiction" data-href="#story-02" data-synopsis="Katniss Everdeen volunteers to participate in a dangerous televised competition to save her younger sister." data-title="The Hunger Games" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Katniss Everdeen volunteers to participate in a dangerous televised competition to save her younger sister.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Reaping Day</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Reaping Day" data-img="stories/02"/><figcaption>Reaping Day</figcaption></figure><p>The siren sounded at noon and the whole district stood in silence. Rue's name was not called, but Ana's heart beat as if it had been. Her little brother squeezed her sleeve and did not let go.</p><p>When the name finally rang out, it was the wrong one. Ana heard her own voice answer before she could think. 'I volunteer.' Every head turned, and the cameras turned with them.</p><p>The train to the Capitol was clean, silent and too fast. Ana pressed her forehead against the glass and watched her district shrink into a smudge of gray. Across the aisle, a boy her age stared at his hands.</p><p>'I'm Jory,' he said at last. 'We'll be training together.' Ana studied him carefully. Everyone had told her never to trust the other tributes, but nobody had said how lonely that rule would feel.</p><p>The Capitol rose out of the mist like a city made of glass and sugar. Crowds roared as the train rolled in, throwing flowers that landed on the windows. Ana forced a smile because Jory nudged her and hissed, 'Wave. Everyone is watching.'</p><p>That night, a tired mentor with a scar on her chin sat beside her. 'Smiles keep you alive out here,' she said, 'but only if you know which ones are real.' Ana touched the little pin on her collar and whispered her sister's name. Then she promised herself, again, that she was coming home.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-01">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-03">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-03" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:74"><span class="cover-title">The Fault in Our Stars</span><img alt="Cover of The Fault in Our Stars" data-img="covers/03"/></div><div><h1>The Fault in Our Stars</h1><p class="author">by John Green</p><p class="genre">Romance / Young Adult</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="03" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="John Green" data-cover="" data-ck="03" data-genre="Romance / Young Adult" data-href="#story-03" data-synopsis="Two teenagers meet at a cancer support group and develop a meaningful relationship while facing difficult circumstances." data-title="The Fault in Our Stars" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Two teenagers meet at a cancer support group and develop a meaningful relationship while facing difficult circumstances.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Support Group</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Support Group" data-img="stories/03"/><figcaption>The Support Group</figcaption></figure><p>The chairs were arranged in a circle, which Lia thought was a cruel joke for people who already felt cornered. She sat near the cookies and decided to say nothing at all.</p><p>Then a boy across the room caught her eye and mouthed, 'Worst snacks ever.' She laughed out loud, and the leader looked up, delighted. It was the first time in months she had forgotten to be sick.</p><p>Her name was Hazel, and she wheeled a small oxygen tank behind her like a loyal pet. Across the circle, the boy was named Gus, and he held an unlit cigarette between his fingers. 'It's a metaphor,' he said. 'You put the thing that kills you right between your teeth, but you never give it the power to do it.'</p><p>Lia rolled her eyes, but she was smiling. After the meeting, they sat on the church steps and argued about a book until the streetlights came on. For once, the future did not feel like the only subject.</p><p>After the meeting, Lia and Gus walked to the bus stop and argued about everything: books, movies, and whether the cookies were secretly made of cardboard. He walked her all the way home, though it was not on his way.</p><p>Her mother asked how the group went. 'Not terrible,' Lia said, and went upstairs. Her phone buzzed on the bed. 'Same time next Thursday? Bring better snacks.' She read it three times, hugged a pillow and found the hum of her oxygen tank sounding almost like a song.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-02">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-04">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-04" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:111"><span class="cover-title">The Hobbit</span><img alt="Cover of The Hobbit" data-img="covers/04"/></div><div><h1>The Hobbit</h1><p class="author">by J.R.R. Tolkien</p><p class="genre">Fantasy / Adventure</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="04" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="J.R.R. Tolkien" data-cover="" data-ck="04" data-genre="Fantasy / Adventure" data-href="#story-04" data-synopsis="Bilbo Baggins joins a group of dwarves on a quest to reclaim their homeland and treasure from a dragon." data-title="The Hobbit" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Bilbo Baggins joins a group of dwarves on a quest to reclaim their homeland and treasure from a dragon.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: An Unexpected Knock</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for An Unexpected Knock" data-img="stories/04"/><figcaption>An Unexpected Knock</figcaption></figure><p>Pip Underhill had a tidy home, a tidy garden and a very tidy life. So when a dozen loud travelers invaded his pantry, he fainted, quietly, into a basket of laundry.</p><p>'We need a fourteenth,' said the oldest of them, pointing a spoon. 'Someone small. Someone who won't be noticed.' Pip woke up holding a map and a strong desire to say no.</p><p>By morning the dwarves had eaten every biscuit in Pip's pantry and drawn a very large plan on his tablecloth. Their leader, a gruff old fellow named Thorrin, explained that a dragon slept on their treasure far away in the Lonely Mountain.</p><p>'And why do you need me?' Pip asked weakly. 'Because the dragon will smell a dwarf from a mile off,' Thorrin said. 'But a hobbit? He will not even know what you are.' Pip took a long look at his comfortable armchair, and a longer look at the open door.</p><p>Pip packed in a panic: two handkerchiefs, a loaf of seed cake, his grandfather's brass pocket-watch. He ran out into the rain with his door wide open and chased the company down the muddy road.</p><p>They were already at the crossroads. Thorrin tossed him a cloak that smelled of smoke and pine. 'Keep up,' he growled, 'and do not look back.' Pip looked back once. His little window glowed warm and tidy in the dark. Then he turned, took a breath and followed the dwarves into the wild.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-03">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-05">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-05" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:148"><span class="cover-title">Sherlock Holmes: The Hound of the Baskervilles</span><img alt="Cover of Sherlock Holmes: The Hound of the Baskervilles" data-img="covers/05"/></div><div><h1>Sherlock Holmes: The Hound of the Baskervilles</h1><p class="author">by Arthur Conan Doyle</p><p class="genre">Mystery / Detective</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="05" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Arthur Conan Doyle" data-cover="" data-ck="05" data-genre="Mystery / Detective" data-href="#story-05" data-synopsis="Sherlock Holmes investigates a mysterious legend involving a terrifying hound and a wealthy family." data-title="Sherlock Holmes: The Hound of the Baskervilles" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Sherlock Holmes investigates a mysterious legend involving a terrifying hound and a wealthy family.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Walking Stick</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Walking Stick" data-img="stories/05"/><figcaption>The Walking Stick</figcaption></figure><p>The visitor left his walking stick behind, and Holmes had named the man's profession, pets and favorite pub before the kettle boiled. Watson tried not to look impressed and failed.</p><p>'Yet the stick is the smaller puzzle,' Holmes said. 'The howling on the moor, however, has kept three generations awake.' He reached for his coat. The game, as always, was afoot.</p><p>The next morning, Sir Henry Baskerville arrived, pale and shaken, with a letter that had been cut from newspaper words and pasted onto paper. 'Stay away from the moor,' it read. Holmes held it up to the light and smiled thinly.</p><p>'Someone wishes to keep you away, Sir Henry,' he said. 'Which means the moor holds something worth hiding.' Watson packed his pistol. If a ghostly hound truly lived out there, it was about to meet two very unreasonable men.</p><p>The night train to Devon rattled through the dark. Watson read the newspaper, Holmes read the passengers, and by dawn he knew which of them were lying about their luggage. At the hotel, Sir Henry stormed down in his socks. 'One of my boots has vanished,' he said. 'Why would anyone steal a single boot?'</p><p>Holmes tapped his chin. 'Because someone wishes to learn what the hound can smell.' Later, on the edge of the moor, a long, shaky howl drifted over the heather. 'A real hound,' Holmes murmured, 'does not howl in tune with the wind.'</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-04">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-06">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-06" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:185"><span class="cover-title">Pride and Prejudice</span><img alt="Cover of Pride and Prejudice" data-img="covers/06"/></div><div><h1>Pride and Prejudice</h1><p class="author">by Jane Austen</p><p class="genre">Romance / Classic</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="06" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Jane Austen" data-cover="" data-ck="06" data-genre="Romance / Classic" data-href="#story-06" data-synopsis="Elizabeth Bennet navigates love, family expectations, and social class while learning to question her first impressions." data-title="Pride and Prejudice" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Elizabeth Bennet navigates love, family expectations, and social class while learning to question her first impressions.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Assembly Ball</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Assembly Ball" data-img="stories/06"/><figcaption>The Assembly Ball</figcaption></figure><p>Elizabeth had been told the ball would be dull, and the first hour proved it. Her mother whispered about fortunes, her sisters giggled, and a proud gentleman in the corner refused every dance.</p><p>When she overheard him call her 'tolerable,' she laughed rather than wept, and told the story twice before supper. She did not yet know that first impressions can be wrong in both directions.</p><p>The next afternoon, Mr. Bingley visited the neighboring house, and every Bennet girl lined up at the window to watch him ride away. 'He is very handsome,' Jane said, and blushed. Elizabeth said nothing, but she noticed that his friend never once smiled.</p><p>That evening, she wrote to her aunt, 'I have met a man of such pride that I think he must keep it in a safe.' She sealed the letter and went to bed, certain she would never think of him again.</p><p>A week later, Elizabeth wandered into a bookshop to escape her mother's matchmaking. The proud gentleman stood at the far shelf, holding the very novel she had wanted. 'You may have it,' he said stiffly. 'Thank you, but I prefer to wait,' she answered, 'until you finish pretending to read it.'</p><p>His mouth twitched, almost a smile, and Elizabeth marched out in a fury. All the way home, she composed brilliant insults. By the time she reached the gate, she realized she was still thinking about him. 'I shall have to dislike him more carefully,' she decided.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-05">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-07">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-07" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:222"><span class="cover-title">The Little Prince</span><img alt="Cover of The Little Prince" data-img="covers/07"/></div><div><h1>The Little Prince</h1><p class="author">by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry</p><p class="genre">Fantasy / Philosophical Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="07" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Antoine de Saint-Exupéry" data-cover="" data-ck="07" data-genre="Fantasy / Philosophical Fiction" data-href="#story-07" data-synopsis="A young prince travels between planets and discovers lessons about friendship, love, responsibility, and life." data-title="The Little Prince" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A young prince travels between planets and discovers lessons about friendship, love, responsibility, and life.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Boy from Asteroid B</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Boy from Asteroid B" data-img="stories/07"/><figcaption>The Boy from Asteroid B</figcaption></figure><p>The pilot's engine had died, and so had his patience. Then a small voice behind him said, 'Please, draw me a sheep.' He turned and found a golden-haired boy standing in the sand.</p><p>'You are not from here,' said the pilot. 'Nobody is from anywhere,' the boy replied seriously. 'But I have a flower back home who is waiting for me.' The desert went very quiet.</p><p>The pilot gave him a drawing of a box and told him the sheep was inside. The boy's face lit up. 'This is exactly the sheep I wanted!' he said, and fell asleep beside it as though nothing could be more natural.</p><p>All night the pilot sat awake, watching the stars. For the first time in years, he wondered how many of them had flowers growing on them, and whether he had ever thought to ask.</p><p>In the morning, the boy told the pilot about his tiny planet: three small volcanoes, a patch of grass and one proud flower with four thorns. She coughed whenever she wanted attention and complained about drafts. 'She was impossible,' he said, 'and I loved her.'</p><p>'Why did you leave?' the pilot asked. 'I was too young to know how to love her,' the boy said. 'But now I think I understand.' He looked at the horizon, where the first sun was turning the sand to gold. 'First, I want to see everything. Then I will go home.'</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-06">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-08">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-08" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:259"><span class="cover-title">The Alchemist</span><img alt="Cover of The Alchemist" data-img="covers/08"/></div><div><h1>The Alchemist</h1><p class="author">by Paulo Coelho</p><p class="genre">Adventure / Philosophical Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="08" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Paulo Coelho" data-cover="" data-ck="08" data-genre="Adventure / Philosophical Fiction" data-href="#story-08" data-synopsis="A shepherd travels in search of treasure and learns about following his dreams and discovering his purpose." data-title="The Alchemist" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A shepherd travels in search of treasure and learns about following his dreams and discovering his purpose.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Dream Under the Tree</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Dream Under the Tree" data-img="stories/08"/><figcaption>The Dream Under the Tree</figcaption></figure><p>Santi slept beneath the old sycamore and dreamed of a hidden chest buried near a distant pyramid. He dreamed it again the next night, and the night after that.</p><p>The old woman in the market charged him a coin and said, 'Dreams repeat when they are tired of being ignored.' Santi sold half his sheep and bought a ticket south.</p><p>He walked past fields and rivers, learning that the road is longer than any map suggests. In a crowded square, a thief took the last of his money, and Santi laughed so hard that a nearby merchant offered him a job.</p><p>'Why are you smiling?' the merchant asked. 'Because I have lost my savings,' Santi said, 'and I have never felt so free.' That night he slept under an unfamiliar sky and the dream returned, brighter than before.</p><p>In the port city, Santi met a lamp merchant who sold compasses that pointed to nothing. 'A map is only paper until someone walks it,' the old man said. He pressed two stones into Santi's palm, one white and one black. 'Use them if you are afraid to choose.'</p><p>That night Santi sat by the harbor, tossing the stones from hand to hand. Each time, he felt a small pang of disappointment at the answer. At last he laughed and slid them into his pocket. If a coin toss could make him sad, then he already knew what he wanted.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-07">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-09">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-09" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:296"><span class="cover-title">1984</span><img alt="Cover of 1984" data-img="covers/09"/></div><div><h1>1984</h1><p class="author">by George Orwell</p><p class="genre">Dystopian / Political Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="09" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="George Orwell" data-cover="" data-ck="09" data-genre="Dystopian / Political Fiction" data-href="#story-09" data-synopsis="A man struggles to maintain his individuality in a society controlled by an oppressive government that monitors its citizens." data-title="1984" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A man struggles to maintain his individuality in a society controlled by an oppressive government that monitors its citizens.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Telescreen Hum</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Telescreen Hum" data-img="stories/09"/><figcaption>The Telescreen Hum</figcaption></figure><p>The screen on Wren's wall never switched off. It hummed through breakfast, praised the Leader at noon, and watched him fold his clothes at night.</p><p>He bought a small notebook from a junk shop, a forbidden thing. His pen shook as he wrote the first line: 'Today I thought a thought that was mine.'</p><p>At the Ministry, Wren's job was to rewrite old newspapers so that yesterday's facts agreed with today's. He was good at it, and that frightened him more than anything. He could change a whole year with a single pencil stroke.</p><p>After work, he walked home past posters of a watching face. 'Someone will read this notebook someday,' he thought. 'And if not, at least one honest sentence will have existed in the world.' He slipped the book under a loose floorboard.</p><p>At the Ministry, a quiet woman named Sela slid a folded slip across his desk. 'Do you also hear the hum stop at night?' it said in pencil. Wren's heart hammered. He crumpled it into his palm and, with great calm, swallowed it before anyone could see.</p><p>When the telescreen announced the daily Two Minutes of Cheer, he clapped with everyone, louder than anyone. Across the room, Sela clapped too, her eyes fixed forward. From that day on, every smile Wren wore was a mask he had to remember to put on, and a secret he was proud to keep.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-08">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-10">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-10" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:333"><span class="cover-title">To Kill a Mockingbird</span><img alt="Cover of To Kill a Mockingbird" data-img="covers/10"/></div><div><h1>To Kill a Mockingbird</h1><p class="author">by Harper Lee</p><p class="genre">Classic / Social Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="10" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Harper Lee" data-cover="" data-ck="10" data-genre="Classic / Social Fiction" data-href="#story-10" data-synopsis="A young girl observes racial injustice and moral courage in her Southern American community." data-title="To Kill a Mockingbird" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A young girl observes racial injustice and moral courage in her Southern American community.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: A Hot Summer in Maycomb</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for A Hot Summer in Maycomb" data-img="stories/10"/><figcaption>A Hot Summer in Maycomb</figcaption></figure><p>Scout spent the summer climbing trees and daring her brother to touch the porch of the strange house at the end of the road. Everything felt simple, as summers do when you are eight.</p><p>Then her father took a case no one wanted. Neighbors stopped waving, and Scout learned that being brave sometimes means standing still while others stare.</p><p>Her father, Atticus, told her that you never truly understand a person until you climb into his skin and walk around in it. Scout frowned, because it sounded uncomfortable. But she kept the idea in her pocket for later.</p><p>That night, a lamp burned late in the study window, and she heard him reading aloud to no one. In the morning, the whole town knew which case he had taken. Scout held her brother's hand and walked to school with her chin up.</p><p>At school, a girl named Cecil sneered at Scout's father, and Scout's fists curled. Jem caught her sleeve. 'Count to ten,' he whispered, in the voice of someone who had just learned it himself. She counted to seven before she had to run to the swing set.</p><p>That evening, her father found her sulking on the porch. 'Courage,' he said, 'is deciding to be gentle when everyone expects you to be loud.' Scout frowned. 'Even if I'm right?' 'Especially then,' he said. The fireflies blinked in the dark, and for once Scout let the quiet win.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-09">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-11">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-11" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:10"><span class="cover-title">The Great Gatsby</span><img alt="Cover of The Great Gatsby" data-img="covers/11"/></div><div><h1>The Great Gatsby</h1><p class="author">by F. Scott Fitzgerald</p><p class="genre">Classic / Tragedy</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="11" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="F. Scott Fitzgerald" data-cover="" data-ck="11" data-genre="Classic / Tragedy" data-href="#story-11" data-synopsis="A mysterious millionaire pursues a lost romance while the story examines wealth, ambition, and the American Dream." data-title="The Great Gatsby" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A mysterious millionaire pursues a lost romance while the story examines wealth, ambition, and the American Dream.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Lights Across the Bay</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Lights Across the Bay" data-img="stories/11"/><figcaption>Lights Across the Bay</figcaption></figure><p>Every Saturday the mansion next door blazed with music and champagne, and every Saturday its owner stood alone on the lawn. Nick watched him stretch one hand toward a green light on the water.</p><p>'Come to my party,' the man said at last, smiling like someone who had practiced. 'I'd like you to meet everyone. Everyone except the one I'm waiting for.'</p><p>The first party Nick attended was louder than a parade. Women in sequins laughed on the stairs and a band played on the roof. Nobody seemed to know who had invited them, and nobody seemed to care.</p><p>At last the host stepped forward and shook his hand. 'Old sport,' he said warmly. His smile was the kind you get four or five times in your life. Then he vanished, and Nick realized he had not heard the man's real name.</p><p>Nick wandered through the party until he found a library lined with real books, uncut pages and all. A woman fanning herself on a sofa laughed, 'He bought it by the shelf, you know.' Nick did not know whether she meant it as an insult or a compliment.</p><p>Near midnight, fireworks lit the bay. The host stood alone on the lawn, one hand stretched across the water toward a green light that blinked at the end of a distant dock. 'Come to lunch tomorrow,' he said without turning. 'There is someone you must meet.' Nick agreed before he understood why.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-10">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-12">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-12" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:47"><span class="cover-title">The Chronicles of Narnia: The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe</span><img alt="Cover of The Chronicles of Narnia: The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe" data-img="covers/12"/></div><div><h1>The Chronicles of Narnia: The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe</h1><p class="author">by C.S. Lewis</p><p class="genre">Fantasy / Adventure</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="12" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="C.S. Lewis" data-cover="" data-ck="12" data-genre="Fantasy / Adventure" data-href="#story-12" data-synopsis="Four siblings enter a magical world and help fight against a powerful witch who has trapped the land in eternal winter." data-title="The Chronicles of Narnia: The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Four siblings enter a magical world and help fight against a powerful witch who has trapped the land in eternal winter.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Behind the Fur Coats</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Behind the Fur Coats" data-img="stories/12"/><figcaption>Behind the Fur Coats</figcaption></figure><p>Rainy days at Aunt Greta's house meant hide-and-seek, and Lucy's favorite spot was the great wardrobe in the spare room. She pushed past the coats and felt something crunch under her shoes.</p><p>It was snow. A lamppost glowed between the pine trees, and a faun with a red scarf stared at her. 'A daughter of Eve,' he whispered. 'Quick, before the Witch notices.'</p><p>The faun, whose name was Tumnus, led her to a small house under the hill. There was tea, toast and a fire, and for a few minutes everything felt safe. Then he began to cry, and Lucy asked why.</p><p>'I was told to capture any human I met,' he sobbed, 'and hand them to the White Witch. But you are far too kind.' Lucy squeezed his hand. 'I won't let her find you,' she said, though she had no idea how she would keep that promise.</p><p>Tumnus led Lucy back to the lamppost, where the snow gave way to a floor of coats. She tumbled out of the wardrobe, breathless, only to discover that no time had passed at all. Her siblings stared. 'Narnia?' her brother Edmund snorted. 'You were gone for half a minute.'</p><p>The next afternoon, Lucy tiptoed back to the wardrobe to prove it. She did not notice Edmund following with a smirk, nor the soft whisper of snow under his shoes. Behind them, a cold wind curled through the pines, and the lamppost flickered as if it knew who was coming.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-11">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-13">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-13" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:84"><span class="cover-title">Percy Jackson and the Lightning Thief</span><img alt="Cover of Percy Jackson and the Lightning Thief" data-img="covers/13"/></div><div><h1>Percy Jackson and the Lightning Thief</h1><p class="author">by Rick Riordan</p><p class="genre">Fantasy / Mythology</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="13" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Rick Riordan" data-cover="" data-ck="13" data-genre="Fantasy / Mythology" data-href="#story-13" data-synopsis="Percy Jackson discovers that he is a demigod and embarks on a quest involving Greek gods and a stolen lightning bolt." data-title="Percy Jackson and the Lightning Thief" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Percy Jackson discovers that he is a demigod and embarks on a quest involving Greek gods and a stolen lightning bolt.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: My Substitute Is a Monster</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for My Substitute Is a Monster" data-img="stories/13"/><figcaption>My Substitute Is a Monster</figcaption></figure><p>On the museum field trip, Leo's substitute teacher stared at him a little too long. Her eyes flickered like a stovetop, and her nails looked suspiciously like claws.</p><p>'Give it back, boy,' she hissed. Leo had no idea what 'it' was. But his sword-shaped pen, which he swore he had never owned, felt very real in his hand.</p><p>Leo's friend Gabe knew the truth before Leo did. 'You never get hurt,' he whispered on the bus. 'Monsters never bite you. And you can breathe underwater, you freak.' Leo had thought those things were normal.</p><p>'Grab your bag,' Gabe said. 'My real name is Grover, and we have maybe two minutes before she comes back.' Leo looked out the window at the sunlit museum and saw something with wings circling overhead.</p><p>The monster crashed through the museum doors with a shriek, upsetting a row of statues. Leo stumbled backward and his pen grew heavy in his hand, stretching into a bronze sword that hummed like a tuning fork. He swung without thinking.</p><p>The creature burst into golden dust, and the museum went silent. Grover grabbed him and dragged him to a waiting taxi. 'There's a camp for kids like you,' he panted. 'It's three hours away and the driver is not a normal driver. Don't eat anything he offers.' Leo stared at the sword and wondered who he really was.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-12">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-14">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-14" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:121"><span class="cover-title">The Maze Runner</span><img alt="Cover of The Maze Runner" data-img="covers/14"/></div><div><h1>The Maze Runner</h1><p class="author">by James Dashner</p><p class="genre">Science Fiction / Thriller</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="14" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="James Dashner" data-cover="" data-ck="14" data-genre="Science Fiction / Thriller" data-href="#story-14" data-synopsis="A teenager wakes up in a mysterious enclosed environment and works with other boys to solve the secrets of a dangerous maze." data-title="The Maze Runner" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A teenager wakes up in a mysterious enclosed environment and works with other boys to solve the secrets of a dangerous maze.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Box Goes Up</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Box Goes Up" data-img="stories/14"/><figcaption>The Box Goes Up</figcaption></figure><p>Theo woke in a rattling metal box, remembering nothing but his first name. When the lid opened, a ring of strangers peered down at him and one of them said, 'Another greenie.'</p><p>They led him through a field of tall stone walls that shifted every night. 'Nobody goes in after sundown,' said the boy beside him. 'The ones who tried never came back to tell us why.'</p><p>At dawn the doors in the stone wall slid open and the Runners dashed into the maze. Theo watched them vanish into the shadows, and asked the leader what they were looking for. 'A way out,' he said simply.</p><p>'For how long?' Theo asked. The leader did not answer right away. 'Two years,' he said at last. Theo stared at the walls, and something strange stirred in his memory, a hallway, a number, a whisper.</p><p>By evening, the Runners returned, all but one. The boys watched the gap in the wall. When the great doors began to close, grinding like old teeth, the leader raised a hand. 'No one goes after him,' he said. His voice cracked. 'We wait.'</p><p>Theo could not sleep. He climbed the watchtower and stared into the dark corridors. Something small and metallic crawled along a wall, a mechanical beetle with a number on its back. It was his number, the same one in his head. Its red eye blinked at him once, as if it knew he had finally remembered.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-13">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-15">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-15" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:158"><span class="cover-title">Divergent</span><img alt="Cover of Divergent" data-img="covers/15"/></div><div><h1>Divergent</h1><p class="author">by Veronica Roth</p><p class="genre">Dystopian / Science Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="15" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Veronica Roth" data-cover="" data-ck="15" data-genre="Dystopian / Science Fiction" data-href="#story-15" data-synopsis="In a society divided into factions based on personality traits, a young woman discovers that she does not fit into just one group." data-title="Divergent" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>In a society divided into factions based on personality traits, a young woman discovers that she does not fit into just one group.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Choosing Day</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Choosing Day" data-img="stories/15"/><figcaption>Choosing Day</figcaption></figure><p>Five bowls sat on the table: water, earth, fire, glass and stone. Kira was supposed to bleed into one and belong to it forever.</p><p>Her test had returned a result that no one would say aloud. She held the knife over the bowls, and for the first time in sixteen years, her family could not tell what she would do.</p><p>Kira's family waited in the front row, her brother's jaw tight and her mother pretending not to worry. She knew the rules: whichever bowl you chose, you could never go back. You must give the faction your whole life.</p><p>She looked at the glass bowl for honesty, the fire bowl for courage and the water bowl for peace. Then she closed her eyes and let the blade slip across her palm. Blood hissed on hot coals, and the whole room gasped.</p><p>The proctor read the result aloud and the hall went quiet. 'Fire,' she said, but her voice shook, because that was only part of the answer. Kira's mother pressed her lips together. Her brother stared at the floor. Kira turned and walked out with the new faction, and her mother mouthed one word: go.</p><p>On the train platform, the others sprinted and jumped. A boy held out his hand. 'Trust me,' he said. Kira leapt, landed rolling on a rooftop and laughed, shaking with fear and joy. Whatever she was, she was no longer afraid of choosing.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-14">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-16">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-16" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:195"><span class="cover-title">The Book Thief</span><img alt="Cover of The Book Thief" data-img="covers/16"/></div><div><h1>The Book Thief</h1><p class="author">by Markus Zusak</p><p class="genre">Historical Fiction / Drama</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="16" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Markus Zusak" data-cover="" data-ck="16" data-genre="Historical Fiction / Drama" data-href="#story-16" data-synopsis="A young girl in Nazi Germany finds comfort in stealing books and sharing stories during a time of war and persecution." data-title="The Book Thief" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A young girl in Nazi Germany finds comfort in stealing books and sharing stories during a time of war and persecution.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Burned Library</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Burned Library" data-img="stories/16"/><figcaption>The Burned Library</figcaption></figure><p>Ilse was too small to understand why grown-ups were throwing books on a bonfire. She only understood that the pages curled like dying leaves and that no one was crying but her.</p><p>When the crowd left, she tugged one half-burned volume out of the ashes and hid it under her coat. It was warm. It felt like keeping a heartbeat alive.</p><p>That winter, the hunger crept in with the cold. Ilse's mother taught her to read by candlelight, spelling out words in the margins of the half-burned book. Each letter felt like a tiny rebellion.</p><p>One night, a stranger knocked, asking to hide in the basement. Ilse looked at her mother, who nodded. 'Bring the book,' the stranger said gently. 'We will need stories where we are going, and so will you.'</p><p>In the weeks that followed, the stranger, a baker's apprentice named Elias who had never learned to read, hid in the basement. Every night, Ilse brought the half-burned book and read aloud by candlelight. His lips moved silently along with each word he did not yet understand.</p><p>When the sirens howled, they huddled beneath the stairs. Ilse kept reading, louder than the bombs, until the all-clear sounded. 'Words are a roof,' Elias whispered, 'that no one can bomb.' Ilse held the book to her chest. Outside, the sky was red, but under the stairs, the story went on.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-15">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-17">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-17" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:232"><span class="cover-title">Frankenstein</span><img alt="Cover of Frankenstein" data-img="covers/17"/></div><div><h1>Frankenstein</h1><p class="author">by Mary Shelley</p><p class="genre">Gothic Horror / Science Fiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="17" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Mary Shelley" data-cover="" data-ck="17" data-genre="Gothic Horror / Science Fiction" data-href="#story-17" data-synopsis="A scientist creates a living being, leading to tragic consequences and questions about responsibility and humanity." data-title="Frankenstein" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A scientist creates a living being, leading to tragic consequences and questions about responsibility and humanity.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: A Night of Lightning</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for A Night of Lightning" data-img="stories/17"/><figcaption>A Night of Lightning</figcaption></figure><p>Victor worked through the storm, sewing the final wire as thunder rattled the tower. The table sparked. Slowly, impossibly, a hand twitched against the sheet.</p><p>The eyes that opened were gentle and confused. Victor felt only horror and fled the room. Behind him, the creature sat up, alone for the first time, and wondered what he had done wrong.</p><p>The creature wandered into the forest and learned the world one painful lesson at a time. Fire was beautiful until it burned, and people were kind until they screamed. He hid in a barn and watched a family through the cracks in the wall.</p><p>From them, he learned language, laughter and the shape of a home. He longed to knock on their door. 'Surely,' he thought, 'they will see that I am gentle.' But the world is not always kind to those who look different.</p><p>He crept to the cottage door and knocked. An old blind man opened it and smiled at the sound of footsteps. 'Come in, friend,' he said, 'you must be cold.' For the first time in his life, the creature was welcomed. They sat by the fire and talked until the house was warm.</p><p>Then the family returned, saw him and screamed. A hand struck him, and another. He fled into the snow, clutching a bundle of papers he had found in his coat. They were a scientist's journals, full of details about his own creation, and one name, again and again: Victor. He learned that he had a maker, and that his maker had run.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-16">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-18">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-18" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:269"><span class="cover-title">Dracula</span><img alt="Cover of Dracula" data-img="covers/18"/></div><div><h1>Dracula</h1><p class="author">by Bram Stoker</p><p class="genre">Gothic Horror</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="18" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Bram Stoker" data-cover="" data-ck="18" data-genre="Gothic Horror" data-href="#story-18" data-synopsis="A group of people confronts Count Dracula, a mysterious nobleman with a terrifying supernatural secret." data-title="Dracula" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A group of people confronts Count Dracula, a mysterious nobleman with a terrifying supernatural secret.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Castle Road</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Castle Road" data-img="stories/18"/><figcaption>The Castle Road</figcaption></figure><p>The coachman refused to drive any further, crossing himself as the wolves began to sing. Jonathan climbed down with his bag and watched the lights vanish behind him.</p><p>A tall figure waited in the castle door with a lamp. 'Welcome,' said the Count. 'Enter freely, and leave something of the happiness you bring.' Jonathan noticed that the host cast no shadow.</p><p>The dinner was strange and silent. The Count served no food and drank no wine, and Jonathan's questions about the castle's old library were met with slow, careful smiles. Wolves howled far below the window.</p><p>When Jonathan finally excused himself, the Count bowed. 'Sleep well,' he said. 'And do not wander at night. The castle has many rooms, and not all of them are friendly.' Jonathan locked his door and listened to a faint scratching on the other side.</p><p>In the morning, Jonathan's door was unlocked from the outside and the Count was nowhere in sight. He wandered the dusty halls and found a room full of maps of London and letters in a dozen hands. Each was addressed to the same stranger. He felt cold.</p><p>In the courtyard, a silver mirror hung on a nail. Jonathan raised it and saw only the empty hall behind him. He ran back inside and wrote in his diary, 'I must leave before dusk.' But when he reached the gate, it was locked, the coach had vanished and the sun was already sinking behind the mountains.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-17">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-19">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-19" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:306"><span class="cover-title">The Shining</span><img alt="Cover of The Shining" data-img="covers/19"/></div><div><h1>The Shining</h1><p class="author">by Stephen King</p><p class="genre">Horror / Psychological Thriller</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="19" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Stephen King" data-cover="" data-ck="19" data-genre="Horror / Psychological Thriller" data-href="#story-19" data-synopsis="A family staying at an isolated hotel experiences increasingly disturbing supernatural events." data-title="The Shining" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A family staying at an isolated hotel experiences increasingly disturbing supernatural events.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Closing Day</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Closing Day" data-img="stories/19"/><figcaption>Closing Day</figcaption></figure><p>The caretaker handed over a ring of keys and a warning. 'Snow blocks the road by November. The hotel gets lonely, and it gets hungry.' Dan's father laughed. Dan did not.</p><p>That night, from the empty ballroom, came the sound of glasses clinking and distant applause. Dan pulled the blanket over his head and kept very, very still.</p><p>The hotel was grand and empty, with red carpets and a long, silent hallway. Dan's little sister, Tess, tugged his sleeve in the lobby. 'The twins in the corridor said they want to play,' she whispered. 'What twins?' he asked.</p><p>Their father said it was only the wind in the vents. But that night, Dan heard a tricycle rattling past his door. He did not look out. He only prayed morning would come before the thing in the corridor got bored of waiting.</p><p>Morning was cheerful. Dan's father whistled as he set up a typewriter in the lounge and Tess drew two girls in blue dresses on the fridge door. 'They told me their names,' she said. Dan took the drawing and folded it into his pocket without looking.</p><p>That night, he woke to the clack of keys downstairs, endless and fast, a single line repeated until it sounded like a prayer. He opened his door. The hallway lights flickered one by one, and from the far end came a small, polite voice. 'Come play,' it said. Dan did not move. Neither did the voice.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-18">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-20">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-20" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:343"><span class="cover-title">Murder on the Orient Express</span><img alt="Cover of Murder on the Orient Express" data-img="covers/20"/></div><div><h1>Murder on the Orient Express</h1><p class="author">by Agatha Christie</p><p class="genre">Mystery / Crime</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="20" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Agatha Christie" data-cover="" data-ck="20" data-genre="Mystery / Crime" data-href="#story-20" data-synopsis="Detective Hercule Poirot investigates a murder aboard a luxury train trapped by snow." data-title="Murder on the Orient Express" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Detective Hercule Poirot investigates a murder aboard a luxury train trapped by snow.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Train Stops</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Train Stops" data-img="stories/20"/><figcaption>The Train Stops</figcaption></figure><p>At midnight the train shuddered and stopped in a snowdrift. At dawn a passenger was found dead in his locked compartment, and the door chain was still fastened from the inside.</p><p>Poirot smoothed his mustache. 'Twelve suspects, one train and no way out,' he said happily. 'Someone here is lying beautifully. Let us find out who.'</p><p>Poirot interviewed each passenger in the dining car: the elegant countess, the nervous secretary, the retired colonel with a limp. Each had an alibi, and each alibi was almost perfect. 'Almost,' Poirot said, 'is the most beautiful word in my profession.'</p><p>He studied a burned note in a washbasin, a woman's handkerchief with a strange initial and a broken watch frozen at 1:15. The pieces did not match. Which meant someone, somewhere on this train, had arranged them that way on purpose.</p><p>Poirot questioned the conductor, who swore that no one had left the train. He examined the snow outside and found no footprints. He examined the passengers and found a strange habit among them: each mentioned the smell of lavender, though the dead man had been horribly allergic to it.</p><p>'Someone wishes us to believe the victim was alone,' Poirot said, smoothing his mustache. 'But a lie told by one person is a lie. A lie told by twelve is a rehearsal.' He looked down the dining car at the faces watching him. 'Mesdames and messieurs, shall we begin the performance?'</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-19">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-21">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-21" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:20"><span class="cover-title">The Da Vinci Code</span><img alt="Cover of The Da Vinci Code" data-img="covers/21"/></div><div><h1>The Da Vinci Code</h1><p class="author">by Dan Brown</p><p class="genre">Mystery / Thriller</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="21" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Dan Brown" data-cover="" data-ck="21" data-genre="Mystery / Thriller" data-href="#story-21" data-synopsis="A symbologist investigates a murder and follows a trail of historical clues, secret societies, and hidden messages." data-title="The Da Vinci Code" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A symbologist investigates a murder and follows a trail of historical clues, secret societies, and hidden messages.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Curator's Last Message</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Curator's Last Message" data-img="stories/21"/><figcaption>The Curator's Last Message</figcaption></figure><p>The museum curator had arranged his own body beneath the great painting, one arm outstretched, a string of numbers scrawled in ink beside him.</p><p>Professor Reyes knelt and stared. 'It isn't a dying message,' she said slowly. 'It's an invitation.' Behind her, the security alarm began to wail.</p><p>Reyes and the young detective, Okoye, slipped through the museum's back corridor. The numbers on the floor turned out to be a coordinate, and beneath the painting a small brass plate read, 'Look where the eyes cannot.'</p><p>They ran their flashlights over the canvas and found an almost invisible latch behind its frame. A narrow stairway spiraled down into darkness. 'After you,' Okoye said, and Reyes raised an eyebrow. 'Cowards first,' she said, and stepped in.</p><p>The stairway ended in a vault of mirrored panels. At its center stood a desk with a half-finished letter, a cipher wheel and a candle that was still warm. Reyes ran a finger over the paper. The ink was barely dry.</p><p>Above them, a door slammed. Footsteps crossed the museum floor, slow and heavy. Okoye switched off the flashlight and whispered, 'Whoever invited you here wants you to find this, but not alone.' In the dark, the mirrors reflected a hundred versions of the two of them, and none of them were smiling.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-20">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-22">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-22" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:57"><span class="cover-title">The Martian</span><img alt="Cover of The Martian" data-img="covers/22"/></div><div><h1>The Martian</h1><p class="author">by Andy Weir</p><p class="genre">Science Fiction / Survival</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="22" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Andy Weir" data-cover="" data-ck="22" data-genre="Science Fiction / Survival" data-href="#story-22" data-synopsis="An astronaut stranded on Mars uses scientific knowledge and ingenuity to survive while awaiting rescue." data-title="The Martian" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>An astronaut stranded on Mars uses scientific knowledge and ingenuity to survive while awaiting rescue.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Log Entry One</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Log Entry One" data-img="stories/22"/><figcaption>Log Entry One</figcaption></figure><p>I am alone on Mars, and my crew has left. They thought I was dead, which is fair. I would have thought so too, except I woke up and was not.</p><p>I have food for thirty days, air for ten, and a very large spreadsheet. Step one: do not panic. Step two: panic briefly, then do step one again.</p><p>The potato plan was my own idea. I had a few kilos left over from a Thanksgiving dinner we never got to have, a little water and a lot of human waste, which I will not describe in detail. Let's just say farming is not glamorous.</p><p>On Day Six, a green sprout poked out of the dirt. I cried. I am not ashamed. I named him Gerald, and I told him he was now the most important living thing on an entire planet.</p><p>By Day 12, I had fixed the radio. By fix, I mean I took apart three antennas and a coffee machine and taped the pieces together. It looks like a robot octopus. It may be the ugliest machine on two planets.</p><p>I switched it on. Static. More static. Then a tiny blip, a pattern, unmistakably not random. My hands shook. I tapped out a reply in the only code I could remember: 'I'm alive. Please send chocolate.' Then I sat down in the dust and laughed until I cried, which, I am told, is a normal human reaction.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-21">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-23">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-23" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:94"><span class="cover-title">Dune</span><img alt="Cover of Dune" data-img="covers/23"/></div><div><h1>Dune</h1><p class="author">by Frank Herbert</p><p class="genre">Science Fiction / Political Adventure</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="23" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Frank Herbert" data-cover="" data-ck="23" data-genre="Science Fiction / Political Adventure" data-href="#story-23" data-synopsis="A young nobleman becomes involved in a struggle over a desert planet whose rare resource is essential to the universe." data-title="Dune" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A young nobleman becomes involved in a struggle over a desert planet whose rare resource is essential to the universe.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Desert Planet</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Desert Planet" data-img="stories/23"/><figcaption>The Desert Planet</figcaption></figure><p>The ship lowered through yellow clouds, and Paul saw only dunes in every direction. His father said the planet was a gift. His mother said it was a trap.</p><p>'Whoever controls the spice controls the stars,' a guide murmured. Far below, something enormous moved beneath the sand and the ground shivered like a drum.</p><p>Their home on the new planet was a stone fortress. Paul's mother taught him to listen to the wind, and his father taught him to listen to people. The Fremen, the tall, silent people of the desert, watched the newcomers from the ridge.</p><p>'They wear suits that save every drop of water,' a guard whispered. 'You do not spit here. You do not cry here either.' Paul looked at the horizon. 'Then I will learn,' he said, 'before the desert decides to teach me.'</p><p>That night, a sandstorm swallowed the fortress. In the howling dark, Paul saw a lone figure on the ridge, a woman in a dusty cloak who raised two fingers in a sign. 'She asks if you are friend,' the guide murmured. Paul raised his hand and, after a long moment, she nodded and vanished.</p><p>Below the fort, a deep drum-like thump rolled through the stone, slow and patient. His mother laid a hand on his shoulder. 'Whatever they tell you about this place,' she said softly, 'listen twice.' Paul stared at the empty ridge and wondered how many eyes had watched him arrive.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-22">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-24">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-24" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:131"><span class="cover-title">The Giver</span><img alt="Cover of The Giver" data-img="covers/24"/></div><div><h1>The Giver</h1><p class="author">by Lois Lowry</p><p class="genre">Dystopian / Young Adult</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="24" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Lois Lowry" data-cover="" data-ck="24" data-genre="Dystopian / Young Adult" data-href="#story-24" data-synopsis="A boy chosen to inherit his community's memories discovers the hidden costs of living in a seemingly perfect society." data-title="The Giver" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A boy chosen to inherit his community's memories discovers the hidden costs of living in a seemingly perfect society.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Assignment Day</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Assignment Day" data-img="stories/24"/><figcaption>Assignment Day</figcaption></figure><p>Everyone in the community received a job at twelve, and everyone clapped politely. When Jonas's number was skipped, the clapping stopped.</p><p>The Chief Elder said only, 'Jonas has been selected.' She did not say for what, and Jonas felt the quiet pressing on his shoulders like a snowfall he could not remember.</p><p>The Giver was an old man in a quiet room. He placed his hands on Jonas's back and, for a moment, something rushed in: sunshine, color, the warm weight of snow. Jonas gasped. He had never seen red before.</p><p>'It will hurt,' the old man said, 'because memory is not only light.' Jonas looked at the plain gray walls of the community, and for the first time he wondered who had decided to take the colors away.</p><p>The old man showed him a memory of a sled gliding down a snowy hill, and Jonas laughed out loud for the first time in his life. Then the old man showed him a darker memory, of loud noise and falling soldiers. Jonas wept and could not stop.</p><p>That evening, his family spoke of schedules and neat bedtimes, and Jonas could not explain anything. He looked at his little sister, sleeping in her comfort, and understood at last that sameness was not safety. It was silence. And somewhere beneath the quiet, the world was still full of color.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-23">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-25">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-25" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:168"><span class="cover-title">Atomic Habits</span><img alt="Cover of Atomic Habits" data-img="covers/25"/></div><div><h1>Atomic Habits</h1><p class="author">by James Clear</p><p class="genre">Self-Help / Personal Development</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="25" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="James Clear" data-cover="" data-ck="25" data-genre="Self-Help / Personal Development" data-href="#story-25" data-synopsis="This book explains how small, consistent changes in daily routines can help people develop better habits and improve over time." data-title="Atomic Habits" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>This book explains how small, consistent changes in daily routines can help people develop better habits and improve over time.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: One Percent Better</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for One Percent Better" data-img="stories/25"/><figcaption>One Percent Better</figcaption></figure><p>Marco wanted to run a marathon and could barely finish one block. He hung a motivational poster and made a grand plan. By Friday, the poster was the only thing still standing.</p><p>Then he tried something silly: just put on his shoes each morning. Nothing more. By week three, the shoes were taking him outside, one tiny step at a time.</p><p>The next morning, he ran for two minutes. Just two. He did not feel like a hero, but he did feel something: a quiet click, like a puzzle piece settling into place. The next day he did it again.</p><p>By the end of the month, he was running ten minutes without stopping. 'I'm not changing everything,' he told his sister. 'I'm just changing a little, every day.' She laughed, because that was exactly how a mountain gets climbed.</p><p>Marco marked every morning run on a paper calendar. On the eighth day, he overslept and felt the old voice whisper that he had ruined everything. Instead, he laced his shoes that evening. 'Never miss twice,' he told himself.</p><p>His sister watched him stretch on the porch. 'I never thought you would last,' she admitted. Marco smiled. The marathon was still a year away, and he was nowhere near ready. But the next ten minutes were enough, and that was exactly the point.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-24">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-26">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-26" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:205"><span class="cover-title">The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People</span><img alt="Cover of The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People" data-img="covers/26"/></div><div><h1>The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People</h1><p class="author">by Stephen R. Covey</p><p class="genre">Self-Help / Leadership</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="26" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Stephen R. Covey" data-cover="" data-ck="26" data-genre="Self-Help / Leadership" data-href="#story-26" data-synopsis="A guide to developing personal effectiveness through principles such as responsibility, prioritization, and cooperation." data-title="The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>A guide to developing personal effectiveness through principles such as responsibility, prioritization, and cooperation.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Between Stimulus and Response</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Between Stimulus and Response" data-img="stories/26"/><figcaption>Between Stimulus and Response</figcaption></figure><p>Dana's manager snapped at her in a meeting, and Dana felt the old heat rise. Usually she snapped back. This time she noticed the gap between what happened and what she would do next.</p><p>'I can choose,' she realized. She took a breath, answered calmly and watched the whole room relax. Small gaps can hold very large changes.</p><p>That afternoon, Dana made a list of the things she truly wanted: a calmer mind, a career she respected, time for her family. Then she looked at her calendar, which was filled with things that did not belong on that list.</p><p>She crossed out four meetings and wrote 'Think' in the space. It felt strange and slightly rebellious. But when the week ended, she had finished a project she had been dreaming about for a year.</p><p>At lunch, a mentor named Mr. Ibarra asked her a strange question. 'What do you want people to say at your retirement party?' Dana laughed, then went quiet. She grabbed a napkin and began to write, and the words came faster than she expected.</p><p>Back at her desk, she opened her calendar and put the important things in first: family dinner, the project, a walk each morning. The small tasks could fit around them, like sand around stones. It was not magic, but by Friday her week felt like her own.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-25">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-27">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-27" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:242"><span class="cover-title">How to Win Friends and Influence People</span><img alt="Cover of How to Win Friends and Influence People" data-img="covers/27"/></div><div><h1>How to Win Friends and Influence People</h1><p class="author">by Dale Carnegie</p><p class="genre">Self-Help / Communication</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="27" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Dale Carnegie" data-cover="" data-ck="27" data-genre="Self-Help / Communication" data-href="#story-27" data-synopsis="This book presents practical principles for improving communication, building relationships, and working effectively with others." data-title="How to Win Friends and Influence People" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>This book presents practical principles for improving communication, building relationships, and working effectively with others.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: The Name on the Badge</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for The Name on the Badge" data-img="stories/27"/><figcaption>The Name on the Badge</figcaption></figure><p>At the networking event, Paolo tried to impress everyone and impressed no one. People nodded politely and drifted toward the buffet.</p><p>Then he tried something different. He asked a stranger a real question and listened to the whole answer. He remembered her name afterwards. She remembered his, and she remembered how it felt to be heard.</p><p>At the next gathering, Paolo asked a quiet engineer about his daughter's piano recital. The man's face softened and he talked for ten minutes without stopping. Paolo did not interrupt once.</p><p>On the way out, the engineer gripped his hand. 'You're the first person who asked,' he said. Paolo smiled. He had not given a single speech or told a single clever story, and yet he had never been so well liked.</p><p>The next day, Paolo had to meet a difficult client who had a reputation for chewing through contractors. He began with something unexpected: 'Thank you for being honest in your last email. It helped us a lot.' The client blinked, then softened.</p><p>That evening, Paolo wrote three lines in his notebook: listen first, remember names, praise honestly. He realized that charm had never been the point. Interest was. He closed the book and went to call his mother, whom he had not really listened to in months.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-26">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-28">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-28" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:279"><span class="cover-title">Rich Dad Poor Dad</span><img alt="Cover of Rich Dad Poor Dad" data-img="covers/28"/></div><div><h1>Rich Dad Poor Dad</h1><p class="author">by Robert T. Kiyosaki</p><p class="genre">Personal Finance / Education</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="28" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Robert T. Kiyosaki" data-cover="" data-ck="28" data-genre="Personal Finance / Education" data-href="#story-28" data-synopsis="The author shares lessons about financial literacy, assets, liabilities, and different approaches to managing money." data-title="Rich Dad Poor Dad" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>The author shares lessons about financial literacy, assets, liabilities, and different approaches to managing money.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Two Ways to Think About Money</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Two Ways to Think About Money" data-img="stories/28"/><figcaption>Two Ways to Think About Money</figcaption></figure><p>Two uncles gave Jun two very different pieces of advice. 'Work hard and save,' said the first. 'Make your money work for you,' said the second.</p><p>Jun drew two columns on a napkin, one for things that put money in his pocket and one for things that took it out. It was the first financial lesson that made sense.</p><p>Jun's first investment was small: a used bicycle that he rented out to friends on weekends. It earned him twenty dollars. It was not much, but he had earned it while sleeping, and that thought hit him hard.</p><p>'That is the difference,' his second uncle said. 'The poor trade hours for money. The wise trade ideas and time.' Jun wrote it on the napkin and tucked it into his notebook, where it stayed for years.</p><p>Jun tracked every peso for a month. He discovered he was spending a surprising amount on subscriptions he had forgotten and snacks he did not even enjoy. He cancelled the subscriptions and put the savings into a small fund.</p><p>His second uncle nodded when he heard. 'Your first asset is not the bicycle,' he said. 'It is the habit of noticing.' Jun smiled and drew a third column on the napkin, labeled 'Things I am learning.' It was already the longest list.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-27">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-29">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-29" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:316"><span class="cover-title">The Diary of a Young Girl</span><img alt="Cover of The Diary of a Young Girl" data-img="covers/29"/></div><div><h1>The Diary of a Young Girl</h1><p class="author">by Anne Frank</p><p class="genre">Biography / Historical Nonfiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="29" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Anne Frank" data-cover="" data-ck="29" data-genre="Biography / Historical Nonfiction" data-href="#story-29" data-synopsis="Anne Frank's diary documents her thoughts, experiences, and hopes while hiding with her family during World War II." data-title="The Diary of a Young Girl" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Anne Frank's diary documents her thoughts, experiences, and hopes while hiding with her family during World War II.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Dear Kitty</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Dear Kitty" data-img="stories/29"/><figcaption>Dear Kitty</figcaption></figure><p>The notebook arrived on her birthday, wrapped in checkered cloth. A year later the family vanished behind a bookcase into rooms no one outside knew existed.</p><p>She named the notebook 'Kitty' and told it everything: the whispers, the shared meals, the hope. 'Paper is more patient than people,' she wrote, and began again each morning.</p><p>In the Secret Annex, the days moved like slow clocks. The adults whispered, the radio murmured and the sun was a thing seen through a curtain. Anne wrote about it all, the quarrels, the jokes, the longing for open sky.</p><p>She wrote about the girl she was and the woman she hoped to become. 'I want to go on living even after my death,' she confided. Every morning, she dipped her pen again, determined that her voice would not be shut inside a wall.</p><p>Some nights the sirens sounded, and the Annex held its breath. Anne counted the pigeons on the roof, memorized the creaks of the stairs and sketched the single chestnut tree she could see through the attic window. Its leaves were the only clock she trusted.</p><p>One evening she wrote, 'Today I was angry, then funny, then brave, in that order.' It made her smile. Outside, the world was still dangerous, but inside the little notebook, she was free. She blew on the ink, closed the cover and slept.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-28">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><a class="btn" href="#story-30">Next story</a></nav></article></section><section id="story-30" class="story-wrap"><article class="story"><header class="story-head"><div class="cover" style="--h:353"><span class="cover-title">A Brief History of Time</span><img alt="Cover of A Brief History of Time" data-img="covers/30"/></div><div><h1>A Brief History of Time</h1><p class="author">by Stephen Hawking</p><p class="genre">Science / Nonfiction</p><div class="actions"><button aria-pressed="false" class="btn like" data-id="30" type="button">Like <span class="count"></span></button><button aria-expanded="false" class="btn ghost syn-toggle" type="button">Show synopsis</button><button class="btn ghost details" data-author="Stephen Hawking" data-cover="" data-ck="30" data-genre="Science / Nonfiction" data-href="#story-30" data-synopsis="Stephen Hawking introduces major ideas about the universe, including black holes, space, time, and the origins of the cosmos." data-title="A Brief History of Time" type="button">Book details</button><button class="btn ghost size-btn" type="button">Text size</button></div></div></header><section class="synopsis" hidden=""><h2>Synopsis</h2><p>Stephen Hawking introduces major ideas about the universe, including black holes, space, time, and the origins of the cosmos.</p></section><section class="reading"><h2>Chapter 1: Looking Up</h2><figure><img alt="Illustration for Looking Up" data-img="stories/30"/><figcaption>Looking Up</figcaption></figure><p>Every night for as long as people have existed, someone has looked up and asked how it all began. For most of history, the answers were stories.</p><p>Then the questions became equations. A telescope here, a theory there, and the universe turned out to have a beginning, a shape and a stranger future than anyone had guessed.</p><p>The first astronomers drew circles, believing the sky was a perfect machine. Then a telescope found moons around Jupiter, and the machine cracked. The heavens turned out to be messier and more beautiful than anyone had dared to imagine.</p><p>Today we know the universe is expanding, that stars are born and die, and that even time bends near a black hole. Each answer opens a better question. And that, perhaps, is the most wonderful discovery of all.</p><p>Imagine a clock placed near a black hole. To someone far away, its ticks would stretch out, slower and slower, until the final second seemed to last forever. Time, it turns out, is not a river that flows the same everywhere. It bends, like a stretched sheet.</p><p>That is the strange lesson of modern physics: space and time are woven together, and gravity is the shape of that weave. We cannot see it with our eyes, but we can calculate it, test it and watch it come true. Looking up is the oldest science there is, and it has only just begun.</p><p class="end">End of Chapter 1</p></section><nav aria-label="Story navigation" class="pager"><a class="btn ghost" href="#story-29">Previous</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#library">Library</a><span></span></nav></article></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><p>© 2026 Pagebound</p></footer><script>
